{
    "type": "errors",
    "wrapper": "errors",
    "strict_fields": true,
    "notes": [
        "Transcribed from the backend app's ErrorsBatchController (validator rules plus ALLOWED_ERROR_FIELDS), verified 2026-08-19; the legacy laravel_version spelling was removed from the allow-list on 2026-08-21.",
        "This is the only type with a strict allow-list: an item carrying any key outside `fields` is refused, which costs that item alone: the server counts it as failed and stores the rest of the batch. The retired Laravel SDK spelling `laravel_version` is such a key now: it was tolerated at ingest while deployed SDKs still sent it, and both SDKs ship the generic framework pair.",
        "The canonical SDK shape is the 23 keys in `fields`, all always present, nullable ones sent as null. The `minimal` example is what the app accepts, not what any SDK sends.",
        "The `full` example populates every field at once so a consumer exercises every rule in one POST. No single SDK run produces it: `headers`, `url` and `method` are null in a console context and `console_command`, `console_arguments` are null in a web context. The `console` example shows the real console shape.",
        "`user` is null or an object with only the keys `id` and `email` (Laravel's `array:id,email`); any other key refuses the item. `user.id` is required whenever `user` is not null, as an integer or a string of at most 255 characters. `user.email` is whatever the host's user record holds, so it is a string of at most 255 characters with no address-format check, and it is null unless the host opted in to sending it.",
        "`message` must be present and a string, and it may be empty: an exception thrown without a message is ordinary, and the app stores it as an empty string.",
        "`exception_context` is what the throwable's own `context()` method returned (Laravel exceptions carry one; the wire key `context` was already the source snippet). It is null or an array, a JSON object or a list, of at most 100 top-level keys, at most 5 levels deep (a flat `{\"a\": 1}` is one level) and at most 16,384 bytes JSON-encoded; past any of them the item is refused. `max_depth` and `max_json_bytes` are this fixture's spelling of the backend's two custom bounds. The SDKs hold it tighter than that, as headroom: at most 50 top-level keys, 3 levels (a deeper value becomes the string `[Max depth exceeded]`), string values of at most 500 characters, and 8,192 bytes encoded, reached by dropping trailing top-level keys, with null when not even the first key fits. Secret keys and secrets in URL values are masked first. It does not take part in grouping.",
        "`frames` is the throwable's `getTrace()` as structured frames, innermost first, and `previous` its `getPrevious()` chain, nearest first, flattened to at most 5 entries with no nested `previous`. A frame carries exactly `index`, `file`, `line`, `class`, `function`, `type`, `is_vendor` and `source`: any other key, `args` in particular, refuses the item, and the SDKs never read a frame's arguments at all. A previous exception carries exactly `type`, `message`, `file`, `line`, `source`, `frames` and `frames_omitted`; its `message` must be present and may be empty, and its `source` is the snippet at its own throw site, vendor or not.",
        "A list holds at most 50 frames. The SDKs keep the 40 innermost and the 10 outermost of a longer trace and drop the middle; each kept frame keeps its original `index`, so `index` is distinct and strictly increasing (`increasing`) and a gap shows where the middle went. `frames_omitted` counts the frames dropped, 0 when none were, and is null exactly when `frames` is (`null_with`). The examples are abridged: they keep a few frames from each end where a real capped list keeps 50.",
        "A `source` is `start_line`, the 1-based file line of `lines[0]`, and `lines`, 1 to 11 entries: up to five lines before the frame's line, the line, up to five after, clamped at the file's ends, dedented together, right-trimmed and secret-scrubbed, each at most 2,015 characters (2,000 plus the `... (truncated)` suffix). Only a frame in the application's own code carries one: a frame under the vendor directory with a non-null `source` refuses the item (`null_when_true`). A blank line arrives as null after the API's empty-string conversion and is stored as the empty string.",
        "Source lines are on by default and switched off with `errors.capture_source` (`RANETRACE_ERRORS_CAPTURE_SOURCE=false`): then every `source` is null and so are the item's `context` and `highlight_line`. Over the per-item byte budget, an error item sheds its frames' sources, then frames from the middle, then previous exceptions, before the generic per-field cut, so no `_truncated` marker ever lands inside `frames` or `previous` (see `client_item_policy` in `envelope.json`)."
    ],
